        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Creator agreements and legal requirements</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">A written agreement is required before running whitelisted ads</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Must specify: usage rights, duration, exclusivity, platforms, payment</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">FTC/ASA disclosure requirements apply &mdash; "Paid Partnership" label is mandatory</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What the agreement must cover</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Usage rights.</strong> Explicitly state that the brand has permission to run paid ads from the creator's account. Specify which platforms (TikTok, Meta, or both).</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Duration.</strong> How long the brand can run ads using the creator's account. Common terms: 30 days, 60 days, 90 days, or ongoing with cancellation notice. TikTok Spark Ad authorization codes have their own expiry (7, 30, or 60 days).</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Exclusivity.</strong> Whether the creator can do whitelisting deals with competing brands during the contract period. Exclusivity costs more but prevents the creator from appearing in competitor ads simultaneously.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Content ownership.</strong> Who owns the content after the contract. Typically the creator owns the content but grants the brand a license to use it for ads during the agreed period.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Payment terms.</strong> Flat fee, performance bonus, or combination. Payment schedule (upfront, on delivery, monthly). Clear deliverables and revision policy.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Disclosure compliance.</strong> The agreement should confirm that both parties will comply with FTC (US), ASA (UK), or applicable advertising disclosure regulations. Platform-native labels ("Paid Partnership") handle most requirements, but the agreement should address this explicitly.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Disclosure requirements</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Both the FTC (United States) and ASA (United Kingdom) require clear disclosure when a paid partnership exists. Platform-native labels &mdash; TikTok's "Sponsored" badge and Meta's "Paid Partnership" label &mdash; satisfy most requirements. However, if the ad appears outside these platforms (e.g., repurposed to YouTube), separate disclosure may be needed.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

